{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1508029-schadenfange","id":1508029,"title":"Schadenfange","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-04-11T07:17:21-07:00","id":1508029,"title":"Schadenfange","content":"How do you all conceptualize emotion in your roleplaying as a vampire? I know it's a simple question, but it seems fundamental. <br /> <br /> What do you think immortal ennui is like? How do you think seeing an unchanging face in the mirror would affect you everyday, or the monotony of only ever standing under the stars? What is undead love? <br /> <br /> With these questions, I personally try to use the Beast as a reference point. It must color all feelings. I would imagine the Beast is kinda like a bulwark against boredom. It will always agitate you into some kind of action. Do you think it filters all passion and mundanity through a lens of violence? I think so. Love seems like it would be something to be feared as a potential kindling to flame in any domain. How does one even imagine the rage of the Beast? I know what it's like to be in conflict as a human, to feel your body tense in anticipation of possible violence as everything slows down and your heart beats faster, but how many times does that need to be multiplied to approximate the nature of Kindred, especially with no heartbeat to speak of? And how does one conceptualize terror as an undead monster? <br /> <br /> Of course, you also have the subjective perspectives of individual clans. If you were Hecata and could see the world beyond death, what would that do to you, make you more appreciative of life and/or cheapen it for you? I can't even imagine roleplaying a Malkavian; how do you even fathom insanity + the Beast and its incessant hunger? I can't, but I wish I could. And how are the Brujah even still in existence with such a dangerous bane? I would assume self-control must be the first and most foremost thing taught to each Childe. <br /> <br /> How do you think vampires feel?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27842-shovelheart3","name":"ShovelHeart3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27842_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"53"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-11T11:16:09-07:00","id":1508056,"title":"","content":"This is one of those topics that I tend to have a different starting point than most since I look at the setting and the context of supernatural existence first, followed by the reality of how the embrace mechanics allow for trait selection, and then finally I look at the concept of character creation in a gaming sense. I bring this up because many people seem to think in the reverse order.<br /> <br /> The WoD does not forgive error, weakness is punished with death or worse. Think of the WoD as a war movie, at the beginning all the characters are fresh faced recruits who have all these preconceived notions about what war is. Fast forward to the end of the movie and the incompetent and unlucky are dead, the survivors are hardened veterans who know what the reality is and what is required to survive on the battlefield. By virtue of the setting, anyone who is susceptible to emotional damage won't survive the first few decades, let alone centuries or millennia.<br /> <br /> Next let's look at the Embrace and how it skews the traits of the vampire population as a whole. Every clan has a set of standards which they value and look for in their potential candidates for the embrace. The ventrue look for competency in business, the assamites look for combat and assassination potential, the Tremere look for mystical skills, etc. But despite all of those clan-centric standards, all of them are survivors of the existential battlefield long enough to prove competent and earn the boon of siring a new vampire. So, this means you will look for candidates which have a high likelihood of surviving such a competitive environment, have the traits that the clan is looking for, and finally have some traits that that will bring the sire a benefit.<br /> <br /> Finally, there is the notion of character creation in a gaming sense. This is the part where a Player sees a character from a different piece of media and wants to play around with the concept or some aspect of it. This is what RPGs are all about, which is great. But in reality, the WoD as a setting doesn't really allow many characters to survive. Most people seem to look at character creation first, then reverse engineer a backstory, and finally look at the mechanics and figure out how to win at combat, or be a social character build, etc.<br /> <br /> Another way to look at the question of emotional processing of vampires, is to look at the difference of how a teenager processes their emotions verses how someone in their 40s-50s process their emotions. One is feeling everything turned up to 11, while the other has had the highs and lows ground down by experience. Given enough time you begin to understand what truly matters and what is just background noise. In the end you prioritize survival over ethics, you value long term security over short term gains, and you focus on gaining additional skills and knowledge to boost your chances of having an edge in any given situation.<br /> <br /> Will the Beast complicate all of this, absolutely it will. But the default mentality of the average vampire will be moved to one corner of a data set chart rather than being in a neutral starting position simply by virtue of the environmental factors of surviving the setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-12T13:46:24-07:00","id":1508195,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar727_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"727\" data-vbnamecard=\"727\">Thoth</a>Very nice post with a lot of contextual illustration. With your train of thought, one could presume vampires experience a relatively hollow existence primarily centered around the pursuit of power; is that your general position? Because I do agree with you on the &quot;seen it, heard it, done it&quot; effect and coupled with an inevitable desensitization to not just violence, but every other passion would seemingly, like you said, flatten out the troughs and peaks. So, I feel like we've got the first part of the puzzle, the human perspective; however, vampires are not wholly human. We have to account for the Beast and all the vigor it confers. Do you think that there is an internal battle between the Man and the Beast, in which the Man produces ennui, wisdom and perspective, while the Beast is a ravenous consumer forever in eternal youth? Because with your post, which I consider to be very accurate of humanity and my perception of the Beast, I would surmise that there is a constant internal struggle for emotional equilibrium within each vampire. So, I think the experiential peaks and troughs are actually both lower and higher for Kindred. It's almost like growing old, but instead of death, you just breakthrough to a new phase of youth because this is what the Beast demands: More. More. More, of everything. <br /> <br /> ?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27842-shovelheart3","name":"ShovelHeart3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27842_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"53"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-13T02:05:20-07:00","id":1508261,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1508029-schadenfange?p=1508056#post1508056\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another way to look at the question of emotional processing of vampires, is to look at the difference of how a teenager processes their emotions verses how someone in their 40s-50s process their emotions. One is feeling everything turned up to 11, while the other has had the highs and lows ground down by experience. Given enough time you begin to understand what truly matters and what is just background noise. In the end you prioritize survival over ethics, you value long term security over short term gains, and you focus on gaining additional skills and knowledge to boost your chances of having an edge in any given situation.</div> </div> </div> </div>Interesting point. While I think that you are right in general, I also think that there are other possible 'end-states' for vampires other than the generic 'cunning old bastard'. I'm not sure right now what they are (other than 'crazy old loon') but taking inspiration from the military genre is a good starting point","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11490-dogstar","name":"Dogstar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11490_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"381"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-13T07:59:55-07:00","id":1508277,"title":"","content":"My preferred take on vampiric existence is heavily influenced by the old Kindred of the East sub-splat of VtM.<br /> <br /> In that system the eastern vampires have concepts of being a mature and refined monster. The hundred years or so post undeath is called the &quot;running monkey&quot; stage where you are expected to run around reveling in your power, settling grudges from your mortal days, etc. But after that point you are supposed to settle down and start behaving like an &quot;adult&quot;. While mechanically the Keui-jin had a lot of stuff lifted directly from VtM, there was a fluff level expectation to keep refining yourself, to keep learning, essentially to keep evolving as a creature on a physical, psychological, and spiritual level. While that can be seen as &quot;gaining power&quot; by some I feel there is a distinction to be made between power acculturation and personal refinement that just happens to gain power as a side benefit.<br /> <br /> I disagree with the vampiric existence = ennui and mandatory shopping at Hot Topic line of thinking. I prefer the notion that the vampire just functions with a different set of parameters. For example, in most media the idea of the independently wealthy hermit who spends their time tending their garden, reading books, and managing their affairs, is shorthand for a quasi-enlightened character.<br /> <br /> A vampire can do the exact same thing; the parameters just tend to be a little different. For example, rather than maintaining a garden, they maintain a power base of influence in the same fashion. Rather than noticing a particular flower or plant will turn into something special, they are grooming people, businesses, or political movements. The alien mentality that we attribute to the undead is in fact them just seeing human level existence like a rose bush or bonsai tree. It is something to be refined and appreciated, but not something to be overly concerned about if it dies or doesn't reach its potential.<br /> <br /> The Beast just tends to push the vampire when it comes to survival and needs. A vampire with a full blood pool won't have a Beast demanding to be fed, a vampire who isn't in danger won't have the desire to kill everyone in the room, a vampire who is residing in their secure haven won't be worried about the sun coming up. Obviously, this line of thinking it relatively Path of Humanity centric, but it does function with most other Paths.<br /> <br /> Once a vampire has a secure haven, a form of passive income, a blood supply, and perhaps a daylight servant, not much else is needed. Vampires heal so they don't need to worry about medical coverage, since they have an unlimited potential lifespan there isn't the anxiety about doing everything important while you still have time or in other words no such thing as a vampire mid-life crisis, vampires generally don't lose the ability to sire thus there isn't a baby clock to worry about, they don't exactly look more decrepit with age so they always are in their prime socially.<br /> <br /> Basically, a vampire, once established, really can just focus on having a fulfilling and meaningful existence. Yes, you will have to deal with the occasional mortal world upheaval, and every now and then a vampiric faction will get uppity, but if planned for, such things don't really have to affect you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-13T08:27:34-07:00","id":1508278,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1508029-schadenfange?p=1508277#post1508277\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A vampire can do the exact same thing; the parameters just tend to be a little different. For example, rather than maintaining a garden, they maintain a power base of influence in the same fashion. Rather than noticing a particular flower or plant will turn into something special, they are grooming people, businesses, or political movements. The alien mentality that we attribute to the undead is in fact them just seeing human level existence like a rose bush or bonsai tree. It is something to be refined and appreciated, but not something to be overly concerned about if it dies or doesn't reach its potential.<br /> <br /> The Beast just tends to push the vampire when it comes to survival and needs. A vampire with a full blood pool won't have a Beast demanding to be fed, a vampire who isn't in danger won't have the desire to kill everyone in the room, a vampire who is residing in their secure haven won't be worried about the sun coming up. Obviously, this line of thinking it relatively Path of Humanity centric, but it does function with most other Paths.<br /> <br /> Once a vampire has a secure haven, a form of passive income, a blood supply, and perhaps a daylight servant, not much else is needed.</div> </div> </div> </div>So, you see the Beast as a minor accessory to Humanity? Again, I find this view one-sided. A vampire is just an average retiree, then. Take for example this statement:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Beast just tends to push the vampire when it comes to survival and needs. </div> </div> </div>You don't think the Beast will have an influence on how a vampire produces art, the way they dance or how they might think about science?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27842-shovelheart3","name":"ShovelHeart3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27842_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"53"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-13T08:59:38-07:00","id":1508280,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ShovelHeart3</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1508029-schadenfange?p=1508278#post1508278\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, you see the Beast as a minor accessory to Humanity?</div> </div> </div> </div>Not an accessory, but I do see it as intrinsically connected to humanity, it taps into our more primal instincts and less civilized natures. But once again I refer back to the Kindred of the East and Wraith, where the vampiric Beast can influence our feelings, but it is not the sort of possession that the Kuei-jin and Wraiths Shadow souls cause. Or another way to put it is that the Beast is a volume increase on what is already there, rather than being an alternate personality that is constantly fighting for control of the body.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ShovelHeart3</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1508029-schadenfange?p=1508278#post1508278\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You don't think the Beast will have an influence on how a vampire produces art, the way they dance or how they might think about science?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that the Beast can influence the individual's perspective and create situations where they learn new things, which in turn can influence art, science or any other endeavor, but you could say the same thing about going to war, starting a business, or becoming a parent.<br /> <br /> One of the aspects that lends a degree of credence to this line of thinking is the fluff and quasi-mechanics surrounding the concept of Golconda. In the few times White Wolf gave mechanical information on it, it was supposed to be the state where you accept the Beast as part of you and you are at peace with your vampiric nature. The side effects of this are that you are not prone to frenzy or other vampiric altered mental states, and that you are no longer capped by your Generation for stat and discipline dots.<br /> <br /> Now Golconda is one of the least refined mechanics in the game system. But the broad strokes of it leaves the Beast as a part of you rather than an external entity that took up residence inside your mind/soul.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-13T09:30:12-07:00","id":1508281,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1508029-schadenfange?p=1508280#post1508280\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Not an accessory, but I do see it as intrinsically connected to humanity, it taps into our more primal instincts and less civilized natures. But once again I refer back to the Kindred of the East and Wraith, where the vampiric Beast can influence our feelings, but it is not the sort of possession that the Kuei-jin and Wraiths Shadow souls cause. Or another way to put it is that the Beast is a volume increase on what is already there, rather than being an alternate personality that is constantly fighting for control of the body.</div> </div> </div> </div>Right, and I generally agree with this line of thinking and this is at the crux of why I made this thread: abstracting from that. The Beast and Humanity are complementary, therefore, I desire to go beyond solely the human perspective. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think that the Beast can influence the individual's perspective and create situations where they learn new things, which in turn can influence art, science or any other endeavor, but you could say the same thing about going to war, starting a business, or becoming a parent. </div> </div> </div>But what does that look and feel like? Vampires do not age as humans do. They do not age linearly and in a state of decline. It is the opposite; their vitality actually grows. You keep referring to established material, but my position is that the material has a blindspot. It pushes the human idea of aging onto the vampiric mind. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> One of the aspects that lends a degree of credence to this line of thinking is the fluff and quasi-mechanics surrounding the concept of Golconda. In the few times White Wolf gave mechanical information on it, it was supposed to be the state where you accept the Beast as part of you and you are at peace with your vampiric nature. The side effects of this are that you are not prone to frenzy or other vampiric altered mental states, and that you are no longer capped by your Generation for stat and discipline dots.<br /> <br /> Now Golconda is one of the least refined mechanics in the game system. But the broad strokes of it leaves the Beast as a part of you rather than an external entity that took up residence inside your mind/soul. </div> </div> </div>Sure, but Golconda is at its core just self-control. Just as we humans learn to manage ourselves, so do vampires. So, let's either set aside Golconda or integrate it into our exploration of the vampiric condition. Vampires do not age as we do. There is no drop off or decline. Youth never leaves the vampiric mind (IMO). A vampire can become desensitized, wise, empathic, spiritual and whatever else comes with experience, but they do not age. Their body does not produce less testosterone, estrogen or what have you that leads to human decline. It's the opposite, a continual increase of vitality. So, again, this is a potential blindspot in how the writers convey Golconda and vampirism. Eternal youth is a purely superficial thing to them. It only affects appearance in their eyes. To understand and immerse ourselves in the vampiric nature, we have to adequately conceptualize the synthesis between youth and experience. You know how the material always talks about elders being disconnected from the modern world and the younger generations? That's bullshit. The opposite would happen. If cunning is a selection process for survival and if the Beast is most prominent in issues of survival, we can rationally surmise that this disconnect is extremely unlikely. The Beast wants knowledge and it wants experience. That's where the material fails. It doesn't understand youth at more than a superficial level. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27842-shovelheart3","name":"ShovelHeart3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27842_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"53"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-14T00:48:57-07:00","id":1508331,"title":"","content":"I would say that since a vampire doesn't age and decline mechanically, unless you are counting things like the Gangrel appearance degeneration or loss of permanent willpower due to bad rolls, you basically have a video game character which is always progressing, always growing, always evolving, which means in turn the mental health issues we associate with the dark undead existence is really wouldn't apply. As I have mentioned previously, the KotE rules and fluff step away from the enforced emo vampire mentality, and since they were produced later in the development cycle for VtM it could be argued that it was a more refined take on vampiric existence.<br /> <br /> One thing that could be an issue is the notion that the character is locked into the emotional maturity of when they were embraced. So a teenage mortal who is embraced will spend eternity in a peter pan like state, while an elder statesman will always be the more refined and calmer individual when turned into a vampire. Eternal life is not the same as eternal youth. The fluff routinely talks about vampires being static creatures despite survival for long periods of time requiring adaptability.<br /> <br /> The big problem with all of this is that the game isn't a simulation built from the ground up to show how a theoretical vampire society would work, instead it is designed to be a game where you can plug in any type of character you desire and still have the system run. Thus, there isn't any real mechanical reason why a Malkavian who believes themselves to be an original My Little Pony character couldn't survive centuries of strife caused by the most brilliant of vampiric minds.<br /> <br /> The variability of the system to allow silly games with a horror aesthetic or a grim dark survival game is a boon to potential players, but it undermines the realism because all the games are supposed to be happening in the same setting, regardless of how much they don't mix with each other.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-14T21:57:44-07:00","id":1508416,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1508029-schadenfange?p=1508331#post1508331\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I would say that since a vampire doesn't age and decline mechanically, unless you are counting things like the Gangrel appearance degeneration or loss of permanent willpower due to bad rolls, you basically have a video game character which is always progressing, always growing, always evolving, which means in turn the mental health issues we associate with the dark undead existence is really wouldn't apply.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure that's necessarily true and I'm going to be pondering this and the potential mental issues long-term vampirism might bring. I think if you look hard enough there are imperfections to be found.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As I have mentioned previously, the KotE rules and fluff step away from the enforced emo vampire mentality, and since they were produced later in the development cycle for VtM it could be argued that it was a more refined take on vampiric existence. </div> </div> </div>I've never read any of the old material, so I'm ignorant there, sounds interesting, though. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> One thing that could be an issue is the notion that the character is locked into the emotional maturity of when they were embraced. So a teenage mortal who is embraced will spend eternity in a peter pan like state, while an elder statesman will always be the more refined and calmer individual when turned into a vampire. Eternal life is not the same as eternal youth. The fluff routinely talks about vampires being static creatures despite survival for long periods of time requiring adaptability. </div> </div> </div>Yes, there is definitely a contradiction. I do however defer to your survival selection theory as the take that is objectively more correct. That is both the most immersive and feasible. Vampires simply cannot be static creatures, even if embraced in youth. So, I think we as players should put our foot down on this issue and assert that kindred are not static beings. I think vampirism is less about stasis and more about equilibrium.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The big problem with all of this is that the game isn't a simulation built from the ground up to show how a theoretical vampire society would work, instead it is designed to be a game where you can plug in any type of character you desire and still have the system run. Thus, there isn't any real mechanical reason why a Malkavian who believes themselves to be an original My Little Pony character couldn't survive centuries of strife caused by the most brilliant of vampiric minds. <br /> <br /> The variability of the system to allow silly games with a horror aesthetic or a grim dark survival game is a boon to potential players, but it undermines the realism because all the games are supposed to be happening in the same setting, regardless of how much they don't mix with each other. </div> </div> </div>I 100% agree on this and I think it's a shame. It feels like the game really does sell out on this qualitative aspect in favor of wider accessibility. If I ever dip into creating community content, this would be the foremost thing I address, immersion. <br /> <br /> Anyway, I appreciate your insightful responses and they've helped me think more deeply about the vampiric existence. ATM, I'm trying to synthesize what I feel are the characteristics of youth (passion, curiosity, sensitivity) and experience (perspective, desensitization, pragmaticism). So, I think vampiric existence is an inherently turbulent one and I'm trying to factor embrace age into that. It feels like if an older person, like in their 80's, was embraced, vampirism in my mind should physically and mentally de-age them to a younger state. My rationale for this being vampirism is an affliction of vitality and also that the old age mindset is completely at odds with the Beast and detrimental to survival (because of lack of aggression, among other things). <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27842-shovelheart3","name":"ShovelHeart3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27842_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"53"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-15T04:47:37-07:00","id":1508429,"title":"","content":"I disagree, I think, at least with the first edition of vampire the masquerade, that exploring a kindred society was just important as having a fun game. Such a complex thing would need too many perfected details and it wasn't all there, but the societal devisions of early VTM were just as, if not more organic than, say, the much touted <i>Fallout: New Vegas</i> with it's various believable factions that each make perfect sense within the context they're found in. Vampire's factions make perfect sense within the context they're found in, and the vampire as a figure has always served as an exaggerated entity to look into human issues.<br /> <br /> VTR feels like a game, with it's various factions that should logically wipe eachother out for full control but don't to give players more choice and storytellers easier drama. VTM, at least before 5th edition, is an exaggerated reality.<br /> <br /> So all that said, I don't really treat my vampires as emotionally different from &quot;normal&quot; people put into the same context. Vampires are more likely to sire tougher people, and any anguish about having to suck blood for a living more becomes anguish that they have to get away with that and lie for a living. The biggest bummer is that nights are too short and the territoriality of vampires makes free travel a little too difficult.<br /> <br /> As I see it, <b>Vampires constantly improve </b>in terms of skills and abilities, even where humans generally atrophy, but sometimes they<b> learn the wrong lessons</b>. An unlikely setback could turn them off a particular behaviour, a friendship that went bad can sour there chances at future friendships, getting isolated and then receiving an olive branch from an obviously bad person in such a state can have them leaning too hard on unreliable people. Beyond that there's vampires just getting attached to outmoded behaviours or discredited theories. What behaviours might've made a vampire incredibly successful with the kine 15 years ago might be seen as abhorrent today, and it's likely a lot of the more baffling excesses of today won't age well either. But people are gamblers and nothing gets them going like inconsistent rewards for their efforts. People are usually quite good at adapting, but I think, despite having eternally fresh minds, vampires with their regular traumatic events they experience + the constant march of time will occasionally err.<br /> <br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-15T09:42:32-07:00","id":1508455,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ShovelHeart3</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1508029-schadenfange#post1508029\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How do you all conceptualize emotion in your roleplaying as a vampire? I know it's a simple question, but it seems fundamental.<br /> <br /> Good post and I will try to answer my best.<br /> <br /> What do you think immortal ennui is like?<br /> <br /> I see denial, despair, frustation and solititude being the main problems. You have all this knowledge and power and you can't enact any meaningful change to the world and when you try and you see it backfire on you in time. The Trinity of Michael, Dracon and Antonius being the best example I can think of.<br /> <br /> <br /> How do you think seeing an unchanging face in the mirror would affect you everyday or the monotony of only ever standing under the stars?<br /> <br /> My take is that this would just reinforce how the kindred are in the world but are apart from it.<br /> <br /> What is undead love?<br /> <br /> With each other it would be a twisted cycle of break ups and getting back together and seeing what love you had there became a mockery. With mortals I would say it would scare them considerably. Dealing with the lupines, each other, hunters is easy. But falling in love with a mortal would be scary as they crave human connection and they know by pursuing it they put them in danger from there rivals. Embracing them over time distorts what made you fall in love with them and same with ghouling them.<br /> <br /> With these questions, I personally try to use the Beast as a reference point. It must color all feelings. I would imagine the Beast is kinda like a bulwark against boredom. It will always agitate you into some kind of action. Do you think it filters all passion and mundanity through a lens of violence? I think so. Love seems like it would be something to be feared as a potential kindling to flame in any domain. How does one even imagine the rage of the Beast? I know what it's like to be in conflict as a human, to feel your body tense in anticipation of possible violence as everything slows down and your heart beats faster, but how many times does that need to be multiplied to approximate the nature of Kindred, especially with no heartbeat to speak of? And how does one conceptualize terror as an undead monster?<br /> <br /> The True Brujah certainly believe that the beast taints all kindred emotions and mistrust them. As for the rest of your question I'll leave to others who can better answer it.<br /> <br /> Of course, you also have the subjective perspectives of individual clans. If you were Hecata and could see the world beyond death, what would that do to you, make you more appreciative of life and/or cheapen it for you?<br /> <br /> I would point out clans are not monolithic groups with one mind, but are &quot;People&quot; with there own goals and you could have a Hecate that may respect life more and use there knowledge to right some wrongs. I'd also go by age to further the answer as many neonates might respect life more until they see their loved ones die and see the world change and now they want to make there unlife more comfortable<br /> <br /> I can't even imagine roleplaying a Malkavian; how do you even fathom insanity + the Beast and its incessant hunger? I can't, but I wish I could.<br /> <br /> In regards to the Malkavians I try to be mindful that mental illness is sad, tragic and scary and not funny and there is some good examples such as Prince Rogue in Dark Ages Iberia who madness shows through intricate planning and mainpulations and comes across quite lucid. Or Qasim in the same book who tries to exist as a devoted muslim the best he can but if you abuse a child and he learns of it. Another side will show or in Cairo we have Aziz who likes to talk in riddles, but is well liked by everyone and the wiser amongst the Kindred learn that he is a font of great info. The point I'm making is that a Malkavian mental illness doesn't need to be obvious like say the Joker from the Batman comics.<br /> <br /> My own character doesn't come across as mentally ill but give him a mystery or puzzle and he will follow it and is aware enough that he can have tunnel vision and his mates help with that.<br /> <br /> <br /> And how are the Brujah even still in existence with such a dangerous bane? I would assume self-control must be the first and most foremost thing taught to each Childe.<br /> <br /> This is where suspension of disbeilf would come in regards in this game as we are playing vampires and we haven't been discovered by the world at large and wiped out. Also to borrow a phrase from MyWifeIsScary from another topic. Most people who are embraced usually have above average ability of self control including the Brujah.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> How do you think vampires feel?</div> </div> </div> </div>Depends on the one in question. In Children Of The Night, Vlad Dracul is done with the modern world and retreated to his home and keeps a lonely vigil and misses the nights of old and feels out of place and the same is said with Marcellus Rufus of Dark Ages Iberia and the Lasombra went into topor during the Spainish Civil War and waits for a more honorable time.<br /> <br /> Then you got Helen who seems to enjoy her unlife and works to have it better. At least according to her quote in Chicago by Night and you got Critias in a funk as he learned that his sire has manipulated him for 2,000 years and wondering who he really is now. Then according to the Lasombra trilogy novels we had Montano being doubtful about his background and learning that his tribe for all intents and purposes had died out and that would weigh heavily on any Kindred's mind as they could be the only branch of there family that is still around.<br /> <br /> In other words they would be as messed up as us.<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/redface.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Embarrassment\" smilieid=\"2\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":27361,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","user":"Lysander","date":". 04-15-2023, 03:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-18T17:08:52-07:00","id":1508794,"title":"","content":"I've been thinking about this one. It took a while, and now it's late so, God, I hope it's coherent.<br /> <br /> It's not so much that vampires don't think or feel like humans, it's more that humans are relatively flexible beings and vampires live long enough to adapt to times and situations that humans never get the chance to: that's what &quot;inhuman&quot;/&quot;alien&quot; means in their context. Humanity as humans experience it quickly begins to inform a vampire's experiences less and less. Less than half way through your neonate stage most vampires have been vampires for as long, if not longer than they've been human. For ancillae, the time when they were human is as childhood to an adult. Elders have only hazy memories, like looking back at your early infancy. Good luck getting a Methuselah to remember anything about what it was like.<br /> <br /> The shifts in thoughts are informed by three things; 1) the Beast, 2) their power, 3) their stasis.<br /> <br /> 1) The Beast is instinctual and responds to perceived threats to one's (un)life, and most vampires don't immediately realise they're feeling it at all. In an ideal situation you don't see the Beast at all. It's just quiet little things, like noticing the flush of a mortal's cheeks, being drawn to any signs of vulnerability, stepping away from a flame without conscious thought and the like. It's more obvious in reaction to a stressor. Going deathly still when somebody enters the room unexpectedly or there's a loud noise (potential threat). They start getting terse and bitchy when you surprise them. They really don't like cohabitation, and will instinctively seek to be alone when injured.<br /> it feels like stress, but where humans start feeling our physical biological reactions intensify vampires feel theirs deaden. Their body slips closer to a corpse-like state and the mind tries to slips into a black and white state of survival vs threat of death/suffering. A bit like disassociation, I guess. They literally died, they don't want to remember what that was like or do it again.<br /> <br /> 2) Violence and injuries that humans find terrifying is like stubbing your toe for the undead, leading to shifts in priorities. If Jim frenzies in Elysium and tears your lower jaw off, making a big deal out of it makes you look like a petulant fledgling. For fucks sake, it'll grow back soon; Jim over there has to deal with the consequences of humiliating himself in public which is waaay worse. He's gonna be dealing with that faux pas all century.<br /> Even leaving out disciplines they're stronger, faster, sturdier and that will start to warp the way they look at things. Do you remember being a kid and struggling to get up the stairs, or lift something heavy, and now you can do both those things without thinking about it? Most people need reminding when the things they take as a given don't apply to certain people/groups. So it is with kindred and kine.<br /> (Also, can you imagine how annoying it is to deal with mortals? They're slow and can't bench press cars and if you touch them too hard the fragile blood bags squirt fluids and bone shrapnel all over the place. They're deaf and blind, and socially slow because they can't read minds or auras. Conversationally they just go through cycles of eat-shit-fuck-sleep-die interspersed with mediocre hobbies. By and large their lives are contained in a little bubble of safe, bland, sanitised ignorance that have nothing in common with yours. Sure, you've got your own interesting mortals you like, but it's like pets; My dog is fantastic, my neighbour's barking mutt is a pain in the ass. Or in the words I've heard from some parents about children &quot;I hate kids. Not mine, but everybody else's.&quot; (which also applies to your fellow vampires' childer. Ugh, fledglings.))<br /> <br /> 3) Vampires are eternal, the world is not. Mortals die, power structures shift, societies come and go and vampires are the only ones still there. The only real (technically) permanent thing is your existence and those of your kindred, everything else is immaterial. You are a god, and the world is a game. You will play the game and fight to rise through the ranks - parrying, hitting, feinting... - and then one day you may fall again, which (assuming you didn't fuck up badly enough to die) is just an opportunity to start afresh. Reinvent yourself (new identity, new city, who knows), plot elaborate revenge, take the opportunity to try out some new tactics. Enjoy things while they last and then move on! Yes, maybe you'll grieve, but emotions run their course and in eternity that course is a mere blip. Relationships, too, change. Today's enemy is next century's ally and vice versa. With the exception of those individuals they genuinely loathe, enmity is a type of friendship for the undead. It will be satisfying to finally take down that jackass you've been plotting against for two centuries, but there will be a void in your life when you win and you will miss the scheming.<br /> (Your alternative is to succumb to depression, in which case you're going to end up dead, on the train to wightdom, or napping eternity away in torpor. Just distance yourself from the world you can't be part of anymore, it can't hurt you then.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What do you think immortal ennui is like? </div> </div> </div>Depression, burn out, etc.<br /> People are stupid, they have been for centuries and they will be for centuries to come; your peers are tedious petty assholes recycling the same stupid plots over and over; you get stuck into a rut and need to go looking for something to jar you out of it. Usually results in stuff like in an entertaining disaster (as in it's entertaining for the bored vampire and a disaster for everyone else), upping the personal &quot;difficulty rating&quot; in ones political games, spontaneous childe siring, entering torpor and napping it off for a few decades so you can just drop yourself into an unknown world...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How do you think seeing an unchanging face in the mirror would affect you everyday, </div> </div> </div>You'd probably get over it, to be honest. I see pretty much the same face in the mirror that I have for the last ten years; remembering that one day I'll look in it and my face will be different is what seems weird (though by the time I have that face it won't be strange anymore, it'll just be my face).<br /> So vampires by and large probably won't think anything of it, unless something reminds them. If it bothers you that much, the Tzimisce exist.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> or the monotony of only ever standing under the stars? </div> </div> </div>And I rule it that even if a vampire finds a way to stand under the daytime skies, they simply find it stressful. The psychological and biological benefits of sunshine do not apply to the undead nocturnal predators. The only vampires pretending to be upset about the appearence of the night sky are Toreadors acting melodramatic for artistic purposes. Besides, the night sky changes more than the daytime sky. The sun is the sun. Stars shift and change, planets change alignments, the moon waxes and wanes and changes colour. How about aurora borealis? You will live long enough to observe many astrological phenomena. You can also use a telescope during the night for more interesting sights (...huh. Can you use Auspex to look at space?)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What is undead love? </div> </div> </div>Comes in two flavours:<br /> A) mortal (you fall in love, you fall out of love, maybe you fall in love again some time down the centuries maybe not)<br /> B) Immortal (blood bond. Two intertwined immortal nightmares essentially acting as one emotionally unstable whole stretched over two bodies, incapable and unwilling to be parted. The fictional ideal of love taken to its extreme - selfless and selfish at the same time, obsessive, all consuming, fulfilling, awful in its classic and modern meaning, dangerous, irrational and, frankly, <i>completely batshit insane</i>.)\u200b\u200b<br /> <br /> (I do have thoughts about individual clans, but it's gone 1AM and this post is stupidly long, so I'll post it later.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><sup><i>Prone to hyperbole</i></sup>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","name":"Rhywbeth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26836_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"377"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-18T19:34:04-07:00","id":1508808,"title":"","content":"As for the lack of change, I assume this is reassuring for a while and after that comes the monotony and assuming you don't want to go crazy you do anything, do something risky or get your hands dirty, your heart doesn't beat anymore but your The inner beast must make you feel something with these acts, I believe, which is also why many Kindred do what they do.<br /> <br /> Now, talking about the beast, I think it's not very difficult to imagine, because it's like you have something inside you that tells you to do what you shouldn't just because you want to, you know being selfish or reacting to your own anger, to the at least once in your life you must have felt like punching someone who pissed you off or doing something out of the law, with the beast it would be similar, only with greater intensity and the difference that you don't need to fear anymore if the other person is stronger or is armed, for you this is nothing, it doesn't matter anymore if it's right or fair just that you can. Another point may be that people for you now do not seem like other human beings, but food, imagine that it is a juicy steak, a decorated wedding cake, an expensive wine, whatever you prefer, but at the same time it is a person with life , dreams, people who care about her, but now all that stops her is her moral compass, because the rest doesn't apply anymore. I think it's close to that, but not as a voice, but as a sensation, something primal like an animal.\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","name":"Psicose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27832_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-18T20:02:28-07:00","id":1508812,"title":"","content":"As for clans in particular, I imagine a Brujah still has his goals and his cause, and I think it's likely his sire chose him for that, once he's embraced I imagine it fills him with a sense of power that he can now do anything and that his ideology justifies everything, because he knows (believes) that he is right, maybe later on he will see how dangerous his new power is, how destructive it is, it makes him retreat and feel afraid of what he has become or maybe Seeing it as a resource or simply burying himself in his cause as an outlet, he might as well embrace that and his cause ends up becoming his justification for unleashing his beast. It might vary depending on the cause, but that's treading into dangerous territory and I'd rather not comment on that.<br /> <br /> Moving on to the Gangrel, I would imagine that since their inner beast is more connected to them then they must be able to sense it and maybe even see it in a way, maybe like a wolf, it could be any animal, but I'm going to use the wolf as a reference, anyway the wolf in question will bark when he's hungry, he'll try to bite when he's angry and the Gangrel will see and feel this happening and he'll have to contain this beast every time that happens, but sometimes he won't get what causes the frenzy and the curse, and later on the wolf will need a collar to not get out of control, the Gangrel will learn to hold the wolf, educate him to release him in his enemies or will be in eternal conflict with him trying to keep him trapped only to let him loose when he slips up, this in the worst case can make him look for isolation or give up and let the wolf run free, depending on the circumstances. years go by and the Gangrel accumulates changes, his wolf changes with him, perhaps he is no longer a wolf or does not even look like an animal that humans know, perhaps he now wants to hunt bigger and stronger beasts, or he feels old and tired which would lead kindred to apathy.\u200b<br /> <br /> The Malkavians, what I think could be interesting is that their inner beast could take the form of a second consciousness in their head, maybe a more psychotic and bloodthirsty personality, or more controlling and uninhibited, things like a joker or a Hannibal Lecter talking in your head telling you to do something you didn't want to do, but they are insistent and there are times when you think they might be right, even more so in an environment like the kindreds.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":27832,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","user":"Psicose","date":". 04-18-2023, 11:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","name":"Psicose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27832_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-20T09:32:00-07:00","id":1509048,"title":"","content":"Ok, what my sleep deprived idiot self was trying to say in too many words was, tldr; essentially, vampires have the same emotions and range as humans, but different emotional responses and priorities.<br /> <br /> Clans:<br /> Toreador: The Toreador aren't just being melodramatic when they proclaim the world to be poisoning them with its tedium and hideousness; their weakness and heightened senses make them hypersensitive to sensory input from their surroundings. Some stimulus touches their soul with some unfathomable positive emotion few will ever know, and others are a living hell to endure (your coterie-mate isn't just being difficult when he critiques your jacket+t-shirt combination; you are actually torturing him). Cue apparently vapid obsession with aesthetics and sense. And then you get two or more whose concept of good and fucking awful clashes and the mean girl stuff comes out.<br /> <br /> Gangrel:<br /> Let's leave aside the slowly mutating into a nightmare moster part of their curse; for the gangrel the beast is not in their subconscious, it's a facet of their conscious thought. They analyse the world through a dispassionate lens of threat, predation, dominance hierarchy and control/possession first and then tack on the human opinions and emotions second. See a human in the street and your thoughts, in order, are: 1) Prey -&gt; 2) What is my best chance of successfully eating the prey? -&gt; 3) Oh yeah, that's a person. (and then come normal thoughts like what they look like, what they're wearing, what they're doing, etc)<br /> <br /> Brujah:<br /> Perpetual fight or fight mode. They're always stressed and hurt and angry and they can never, ever turn it off although those that survive learn to mask it. These people have the thinnest skin in the universe, and it's exhausting for themselves and everyone around them. Gentle teasing, even if they know logically it's nothing to get upset about, will cause a deep emotional wound (which translates to rage)<br /> Then there are the coping mechanisms. Looking for excuses to lash out (be it actual violence or emotionally evicerating people at Elysium). Turning into a total black-and-white minded control freak is also typical, be this in their personal or political lives; they'll try to drive out or exterminate everything that upsets them, and minimise contact with these things.<br /> <br /> Malkavian:<br /> Each Malk is an individual, who responds to their situation uniquely. It's very hard to generalise the clan. The only thing they have in common is that they're basically aerials picking up cosmic signals they are in no way equipped to translate and it has a negative impact on their life. Everyone puts you down for being crazy (the second anyone knows what you are is the second nobody's taking you seriously anymore), reality is a shaky concept, you can't tell if you're having prophecies or if you're just experiencing every day vampiric paranoia... It's frustrating, lonely, terrifying, depressing... etc.<br /> <br /> Hecata:<br /> Other kindred get a comforting illusion. Hecata don't. Walking corpses denied the comforting pretense of life. You died fully aware in agony and mortal terror, you inflict that same terror on others when you feed, you've seen Hell. Other clans are romanticising their death; ignorant of a whole other world and the state of spiritual decay that surrounds them; dressing up what they do as &quot;the Kiss&quot; and pretending not to be dead things, and the Hecata tend to perceive that negatively (dismissiveness, contempt, discomfort or outright offense, etc). The Giovanni families exploit that to keep their progeny loyal (Nobody else understands us. Stay with your loving family.)<br /> How they respond to death is an individual matter thing, but whether they embrace or flee from it it shapes their unlife.<br /> <br /> I might have some thoughts on the Lasombra and Tzimisce and their complicated relationship with identity (one is intrinsically tied to nonexistence, the other can reinvent themself until they don't even remember who they started as), but the words aren't really coming to me.<br /> <br /> And some slightly more awake extrapolation on previous blathering about how certain facets of vampirism feel:<br /> <br /> <b>The Beast</b><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> Unless you're Gangrel, the beast is something in the background; it feels like a void that reminds you that you're trapped in the doorway between life and death and you <i>really</i> don't want to pass through it. The rage and fear of frenzy mostly results from the vampire's panic of feeling themself mentally balancing on the edge of the insatiable, bottomless pit of hunger with a dead thing (what they should be) that only wants to eat and eat and eat, and is perfectly happy to eat them.</div></div> </div> </div>\u200b<br /> <br /> <b>Blood Bond</b> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> Vitae straight from the vein resonates with the heartsblood of the vampire it originates from. Placed into the veins of another, the will and emotion of the one it came from begins to intertwine with that of the recipient, who feels it as their own. Mutual blood binding results in this process going both ways, a sort of minor merging of identity in a way. With a full bond bondmates are an amalgamation of individuals, hyper aware of their partner's mood and needs, treating each other as an extension of themselves. &quot;I&quot;/&quot;me&quot; is consumed by &quot;we&quot;/&quot;us&quot;. An insult to one is an insult to both. If one is happy, both are happy. etc. They will prioritise their lover over literally everything else. If the world gets between them, the world will burn. There are no lovers on the planet closer (and more codependent) than a pair of vampires. The final death of one usually results in spectacular murder-suicide rampages.<br /> <br /> Vampire society strongly recommends against the formation of such relationships. Many couples refrain from the third drink, simply engaging in the intimacy and lasting honeymoon period of the for two stages.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><sup><i>Prone to hyperbole</i></sup>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","name":"Rhywbeth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26836_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"377"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-21T09:25:19-07:00","id":1509197,"title":"","content":"Excellent post Rhywbeth.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-23T10:54:33-07:00","id":1509398,"title":"","content":"I'm still thinking about this topic and have been contemplating everything said.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27842-shovelheart3","name":"ShovelHeart3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27842_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"53"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-23T17:23:40-07:00","id":1509421,"title":"","content":"I hope that we gave you enough answers.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-04T20:01:53-07:00","id":1513910,"title":"","content":"Still thinking. Bump.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27842-shovelheart3","name":"ShovelHeart3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27842_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"53"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-05T08:39:04-07:00","id":1513955,"title":"","content":"My take is that the last true connection a Kindred feels, is that of their Sire. After being turned, they are unable to create new relations so to speak, but instead project a previous person on to that. This makes it hard to relate to new Kindred, or to new ideas, because they project someone else. While certainly logic can be used, older Kindred are very stuck in their way. The more people and personalities the Kindred met before their Embrace, the deeper the possible projections are. A Kindred created as a childe or young adult will have much fewer and will have a hard time existing. <br /> Many childer are created from the notion that \u201cthey remind them of someone\u201d, which is a pure projection. The childer does not return that feeling. Thus the situation typically turns sour between Sire and childe.<br /> <br /> As for the beast, I do not think it surfaces as an intelligent being, that would argue or use logic. It is that gnawing feeling in the stomach when you\u2019re hungry. It is that anger that is just below the surface, ready to snap if you let your guard down. It is that fear of the unknown lying in wait. It is always there, like toothache, and it is hard to remember a time before it. It does not need to push, it is simply there when a Kindred fails in their self-control, which they inevitably will.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12225-trassel","name":"Trassel","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"31"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}